# Ownership by area. GitHub auto-requests a review from the owner when a PR
# touches their files, so nobody has to remember who to ask.
#
# Being an owner does not mean you are the only one who may edit a file - it
# means you are accountable for reviewing changes to it.
#
# Most areas below name TWO owners, and that is not decoration. GitHub does
# not count an author's approval of their own pull request, so an area with a
# single owner cannot be reviewed by anyone once that owner is the one writing
# the change - the requirement becomes unsatisfiable and the PR is blocked
# forever. PR #19 sat in exactly that state: approved, mergeable, and BLOCKED.
# The second name is whoever reviews when the first name is the author.
#
# Three sections stay deliberately lead-only: detection core, content, and the
# security-sensitive paths. Widening those to make merges convenient would give
# away the reason they are listed separately at all. The cost is real and
# accepted - a lead-authored change to those paths needs an admin merge, which
# is the correct amount of friction for the accuracy-critical and
# breach-sensitive surfaces. Revisit when a second reviewer exists who can
# genuinely review a migration or a prompt change.

